/**
 * @defgroup DspCore_Example_FiltersIIR IIR
 * @ingroup DspCore_Examples_Filters
 * @brief Inspect a generic numerator/denominator IIR response to a step pulse.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Example_FiltersIIR_Goal Goal
 * Configure a generic IIR with four numerator and two denominator coefficients,
 * including an `a0` normalization from two, then observe its stateful response
 * to a pulse that is high from 10 ms through 40 ms.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Example_FiltersIIR_Run Run and render
 * @code{.bash}
 * ./build/macos_arm64/bin/Debug/DspCore_Filters_IIR_d --output ./output
 * ./build/macos_arm64/bin/Debug/render_signal.sh ./output/iir_step_pulse.csv
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section DspCore_Example_FiltersIIR_Output Output
 * @code{.text}
 * DspCore :: Generic IIR step-pulse response
 * Wrote signal data: ./output/iir_step_pulse.csv
 * PNG: .../render_signal.sh ./output/iir_step_pulse.csv
 * @endcode
 *
 * This is intentionally a time-only view: an FFT of a finite step pulse would
 * describe the stimulus, not the IIR transfer function. The plot shows the
 * normalized recurrence reaching its unity DC response and decaying after the
 * falling edge.
 *
 * \image html iir_step_pulse.png "Generic IIR step-pulse tutorial output" width=900px
 *
 * @section DspCore_Example_FiltersIIR_Code Desktop data generator
 * \include Filters/IIR/Desktop/main.cpp
 *
 * @subsection DspCore_Example_FiltersIIR_Kernel Portable IIR API use
 * \include Filters/IIR/Shared.cpp
 */
